77
00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:18.100
Carly Huitema: that it's ready to go, and I'm going to record it and release it. When we build digital identity systems, we assume one entity controls

78
00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:27.599
Carly Huitema: One entity controls one identity through a valid chain of cryptographic keys. Keys may rotate over time, but the history of control should remain consistent.

79
00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:50.070
Carly Huitema: Two major risks threaten that assumption, impersonation and duplicity. Impersonation happens when someone steals your keys. They can act as you and continue updating your identity, even though you've lost control. Duplicity is different. It happens when the identity owner creates two conflicting versions of their own history, showing one version to one party and another version elsewhere.

80
00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:53.919
Carly Huitema: Both appear valid independently, but they cannot both be true.

81
00:18:54.140 --> 00:19:07.779
Carly Huitema: Traditionally, we solve these problems using trusted third parties. A wedding certificate, for example, includes witnesses who independently confirm what happened. Later, blockchains extended this idea by acting as a global witness. Everyone shares the same history.

82
00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:12.089
Carly Huitema: New decentralized identity systems like WebVH and WebS,

83
00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:17.860
Carly Huitema: Use different, model-based, sorry, brain…

84
00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:37.010
Carly Huitema: derailed. WebS use a different model based on witnesses and watchers. Witnesses are selected by the identity owner. Whenever an identity changes, witnesses record the event and issue cryptographic receipts proving they observed it. They don't control the identity, they simply provide an independent record of history.

85
00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:46.890
Carly Huitema: This helps detect both impersonation and duplicity. Conflicting histories or suspicious changes become visible because witnesses have recorded what they actually saw.

86
00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:52.200
Carly Huitema: Watchers add another layer. They are chosen by the verifier, not the identity owner.

87
00:19:52.200 --> 00:20:15.780
Carly Huitema: Watchers compare an identity's history against the witness records and look for inconsistencies. Together, witnesses and watchers create decentralized accountability. Instead of relying on a central authority, trust comes from… trust comes from transparent records and independent verification. The goal is not to prevent every attack in advance, but to ensure that an impersonation or duplicity can be detected and proven when it occurs.

